{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"{\n  \"records\": [\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S201-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"5de657407c0ffeb60338b9682b7935b835c5340ed654aa9c412976afa8365690\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes repeated deliberate losing with 'Again,' but A3 and A5 praise generosity without making its cost visible.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 acknowledges B2's permission to stop; A5 answers B4's proposed reversal through already-invested generosity.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 rejects a gifted win, and B4 offers the concrete remedy of putting the piece back.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A5 defends the same sacrifice after B4, but adds neither a visible cost nor a further consequence.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 immediately establishes chess, although 'best chess piece' is less playable than naming the threatened piece.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3's 'almost athletic' and A5's investment language are compact but substitute cleverness for the missing cost.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 66\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I've left my queen where you can take it. Same as last Sunday.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I want to beat you properly.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"You can. I've also stopped practising so you can catch up.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Then come back to chess club with me.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"All right. But introduce me as your old coach. They only know me as the person you keep beating.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"S201 makes A repeat a familiar sacrifice that enables B's victory, then insist that B encounter its personal cost.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Generic martyrdom can advertise suffering without actually making another person's victory easier.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Original A1 deliberately exposes a piece again; revised A3 sacrifices practice and A5 exposes the resulting loss of standing.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"This alternative uses an affectionate rival's invitation to reveal damaged standing; alternative b uses a colleague's practical offer to redistribute presentation risk.\",\n      \"reason\": \"The original concealed the required cost. The revision keeps chess and adds a club where that cost becomes public. Separate internal reread: fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 4, heightening 4, specificity 5, brevity 4 = 92. B4's invitation makes A5's request about introductions necessary.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A3 introduces a substantial additional sacrifice quickly; actors must play the request for recognition sincerely rather than as a calculated guilt trap.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S201-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"d1537809091272ae430deae4f653088a424bd5c9f03bc22d95fbd2c0a6c99666\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 absorbs the difficult budget explanation, but A5 emphasizes a 'vacant role' rather than the cost of enabling B's success.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 rejects B2's offer to share difficulty; A5 directly interprets B4's proposed preparation as displacement.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 offers shared responsibility and B4 proposes becoming prepared, credibly challenging A's protector role.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 reveals dependence on the role after B4, but does not heighten the burden established in A1.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 supplies a presentation and missing budget, giving both colleagues a concrete division of work.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's 'effortlessly prepared' and A5's 'vacant role' form a concise, connected exchange.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 71\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I've given you the good sales figures again. I'll explain the missing budget.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"We could split the difficult part.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"You need a clean run at promotion. I can survive another meeting as the budget problem.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Let me introduce your section, then.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Thank you. Say I'm explaining the hole, not that I dug it. Last time that distinction cost me a bonus.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"S201 makes A secure B's easier victory through a repeated sacrifice and then make the accumulated cost explicit.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Needing to remain useful concerns belonging; this card specifically connects another person's advantage to A's sacrifice.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Original A5's 'vacant role' risks the belonging game; revised A3 links promotion to taking blame and A5 names a lost bonus.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a negotiates recognition inside a friendship; this alternative accepts a colleague's help and turns it into protection against repeated professional harm.\",\n      \"reason\": \"The original's final move drifted toward preserving usefulness. The revision keeps the presentation and adds promotion and bonus stakes. Separate internal reread: fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 4, heightening 5, specificity 5, brevity 4 = 95. B4's introduction offer directly enables A5's precise wording request.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The bonus is newly illustrative backstory; the exchange should preserve A's wish to help B rather than imply that all generosity was fraudulent.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S202-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"7df9b97cc0a45a515068080ac944fcc027ac0a43241ff62456f44b6ea91cd816\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 converts a darts win into lunch debt, but A5 keeps B liable even when B4 proposes A winning.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"B4 asks what changes if A wins; A5's borrowed confidence does not establish a coherent changed obligation.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 disputes who gets treated and B4 tests reciprocity, but A5 evades that credible test.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A5 adds accounting language after B4 without making the next result change who owes what.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 clearly supplies yesterday's darts and today's lunch as a playable social transaction.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3's 'confidence is funded' is concise, but A5's borrowing metaphor obscures the joke's accounting.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 55\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"You beat me at darts yesterday, so you owe me lunch.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Fine. But you've just beaten me today.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Then lunch cancels out. I owe you dessert for winning by more.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Make it takeaway. I have to collect Mum.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I'll collect her. A three-dart finish ought to cover a parent, not just a pudding.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"S202 turns successive competitive results into a running balance that changes the favors each person owes.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Entitled score-settling always makes B pay; this card requires new results to alter the allocation of debt.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Original A5 leaves B paying despite B4's reversal; revised B2 reverses the result and A3 makes A the debtor.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"This alternative lets A cheerfully repay a win through practical help; alternative b bargains over a household concession after a disputed sports result.\",\n      \"reason\": \"The original failed the changing-debtor mechanism. The revision keeps darts and lunch, adding a family pickup prompted by B4. Separate internal reread: fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 4, heightening 4, specificity 5, brevity 4 = 92. B4's collection problem determines A5's larger repayment.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The exchange deliberately invents an eccentric exchange rate between a darts margin and favors; mutual familiarity must make that accounting playable.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S202-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"8f501d6c1c9e0bba1696471669dea5d2e7d5b9a698ee96f6ed53a4f2521d4bbf\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 makes a tennis point purchase film choice, but A5 turns hypothetical film dissatisfaction into compensation without a new competitive result.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3 answers B2's rules objection socially; A5 answers B4 but leaves the compensation mechanism unspecified.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 objects to imported tennis rules, while B4's concern about A disliking A's own choice supplies limited personal pressure.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A5 promises tomorrow's compensation without specifying a changed debtor, favor, risk, or concession.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 links a deciding tennis point to choosing a film, an immediately understandable domestic negotiation.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3's 'balance of our friendship' is economical, but A5's unspecified compensation loses the concrete film-choice joke.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 59\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"You won our tennis match, so you owe me first choice of film.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"You won the rematch this morning.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Then it's your choice. Apparently I spent two hours earning your submarine documentary.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Three hours. It's a trilogy.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I'll watch all three. But if you beat me tomorrow, you owe me the director's commentary on my musical.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"S202 carries wins and losses into an ongoing ledger of favors and concessions whose debtor changes with the result.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Ordinary revenge threatens future pain without treating a new result as the condition that changes an obligation.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised B2 supplies A's new win, A3 transfers film choice to B, and A5 makes the next concession conditional on tomorrow's result.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a expands repayment into helpful transport; this alternative accepts an unwelcome film choice and negotiates reciprocal future exposure.\",\n      \"reason\": \"The original lacked a result that changed liability. The revision preserves tennis and film choice while making reciprocity explicit. Separate internal reread: fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 4, heightening 4, specificity 5, brevity 4 = 92. B4's trilogy increases the concession that A5 accepts and prices into the next match.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A5's future result is conditional, although the actual reversal already occurs in B2 and A3; delivery should emphasize that A accepts the current debt.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S203-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"2c526eecf38a4364fdf78f658beb68260388d3c2239b66cd680aa2758a09cbf4\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 makes another rival inadequate and A3 proposes a handicap, but A5 stops at valuing company instead of raising the challenge again.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"B2's four defeats cause A3's handicap; B4's trophy offer causes A5 to contrast polishing it with their Tuesdays.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 gives a credible reason to quit and B4 offers A the prize, forcing A to expose the relational stakes.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 reveals Tuesday companionship but does not build on A3's concrete competitive adjustment.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes a chess concession; A5's Tuesdays give the rivalry a playable recurring place in their lives.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's 'handicap, not a farewell' and A5's trophy-polishing image are concise and connected.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 83\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Don't concede. Who else am I supposed to beat at chess on Tuesdays?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"You've beaten me four times.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Next game I start without my queen. Make me earn it.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I'd still lose. Just take the trophy.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Then take both my rooks too. I'm not spending Tuesdays alone with something that can't gloat.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"S203 makes an uncontested victory feel empty, so A increases the competitive challenge to preserve this particular rivalry.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Letting someone win can seek gratitude for sacrifice; here handicapping A serves the continuation of a valued contest.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Original A5 values annoying B over polishing a trophy; revised A3 and A5 progressively remove A's pieces to keep B engaged.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"This alternative increases A's handicap against a discouraged opponent; alternative b proposes a harder shared baking contest to tempt a retiring peer.\",\n      \"reason\": \"The original was close but did not complete the repeated challenge mechanism. The revision retains chess, Tuesdays and the trophy. Separate internal reread: fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 4, heightening 5, specificity 5, brevity 4 = 95. B4's prediction of another loss causes A5's larger handicap.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"Offering both rooks is deliberately extreme; the scene depends on A valuing continued rivalry more than competitive fairness.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S203-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"1e0e890f14d3d52bc5e948294e7b7929cd57e86e493c82859b6cb13f72f88477\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 and A5 make victory without B unappealing, but none of A1, A3 or A5 raises the baking challenge.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 rejects B2's younger replacements through shared history; A5 answers B4's enjoyable victory with the burden of strangers.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 proposes a replacement and B4 offers freedom to enjoy winning, making A defend this particular relationship.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A5 restates the disadvantage of replacement after B4 rather than committing to a harder contest.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1's bake-off and A3's twenty years establish both an activity and a longstanding rivalry.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's learned irritation and A5's explaining achievement to strangers efficiently reveal attachment.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 70\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Don't retire from the bake-off. Nobody else knows when my icing is smug.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I'm bored with sponge cakes.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Then wedding cakes. Three tiers each. Give me something to fear.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I made those professionally for twenty years.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Five tiers, then, and you choose the fillings. I'd rather lose sleep over you than win another sponge nobody insults properly.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"S203 makes A raise the difficulty of competing because a victory without this familiar opponent would lose its value.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Nostalgic attachment can merely ask someone to stay; this instruction requires renewed challenge as the means of retaining them.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Original A3 supplies twenty years of attachment without a challenge; revised A3 proposes three tiers and A5 raises that to five after B4's expertise.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a responds to repeated defeat by weakening A's chess position; this alternative responds to boredom and expertise by proposing a more demanding event.\",\n      \"reason\": \"The original captured hollowness but omitted the active challenge. The revision retains the bake-off and supplies new illustrative wedding-cake expertise. Separate internal reread: fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 4, heightening 5, specificity 5, brevity 4 = 95. B4 makes three tiers insufficient, prompting A5's escalation.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The proposed five-tier contest may still fail to interest B; it remains an offer with room for further negotiation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S204-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"5ce7e9edbd8e12c69b2500072058020b7deb1beae3cb5d6f71cb0d3a585e61e7\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 makes the Cornwall dispute govern this year's holiday, and A5 extends the old navigation contest to website authority.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A3 turns B2's fifteen-year delay into matured evidence; A5 uses B4's booking proposal as another navigation round.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 challenges the dispute's relevance and B4 wants to book, giving A a present decision to obstruct.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5 makes even navigating the booking website depend on winning the old map argument.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 supplies Cornwall, holiday planning and a map dispute; B2 establishes the long shared history.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A3's maturing evidence and A5's website navigation derive compactly from the same unresolved disagreement.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 94\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Before we choose this year's holiday, settle who won the map argument in Cornwall.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"That was fifteen years ago.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Yes. We've given the evidence ample time to mature.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Can't we just book somewhere?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"After we establish who is allowed to navigate the website.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"retained\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"S204 makes a present decision function as another round of an unresolved earlier contest.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"A grudge can revisit an old grievance without turning today's practical choice into a continuation of the contest.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A1 explicitly conditions this year's holiday on the old dispute, and A5 transfers that dispute into control of today's website.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"This alternative imports an old navigation dispute into a new activity; alternative b physically preserves the original game and bargains over restarting it.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Retained unchanged: B2 supplies the elapsed time used by A3, and B4 supplies booking as the new arena for A5. Rereading the original supports the same ratings and internal pass.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"B's relationship to A remains unspecified, though shared holiday planning and fifteen years of history give actors sufficient footing.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S204-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"31e6d47827a6901a5b6efb29cc768a921795298dccf12e6d6027cbd73746c15a\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 preserves the disputed position and A5 makes a fresh game conditional on conceding the old ending.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 contrasts B2's house moves with the stationary game; A5 converts B4's restart request into a demand for retraction.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 invokes major life changes and B4 asks for a fresh start, resisting A's preserved dispute.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5 appropriates B4's escape route by making starting again another attempt to win the old argument.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes preserved board-game pieces, though neither the game nor the disputed move is identified.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's 'waiting responsibly' and A5's conditional welcome sustain the same compact comic logic.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 86\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Same board game, same disputed last move. I've kept the pieces exactly where they were.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"We've both moved house since then.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"The game hasn't. It's been waiting responsibly.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I'd rather start again.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Wonderful. Begin by retracting your objection to the old ending.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"retained\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"S204 makes A continue an unfinished contest through the terms of a present invitation to play.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Simple resistance to change would preserve the board without using a new start to pursue the disputed old result.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A5 accepts B4's proposed restart only as an opportunity to secure retraction of the previous objection.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a transfers an old contest into holiday booking; this alternative uses a preserved object and a conditional concession within the same activity.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Retained unchanged: both B turns materially shape A's replies, and B4's fresh-start proposal becomes A5's renewed claim. Original reread supports the same internal-pass ratings.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A3 personifies the game rather than explaining how its position survived moving house; that modest comic license remains readily playable.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S205-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"948a0a90ca2b234c181ae1e271da51d61c8f08dc27cdb23d06b5949092b73882\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 copies B's casual success competitively, and A5 explicitly wants the rehearsed response to look less prepared than B's.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A3 reframes B2's lack of a prize as preparation time; A5 uses B4's mirror practice as work toward superior ease.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 and B4 credibly puncture A's claim, although B has little expressed want beyond correcting the performance.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5 turns evidence of strenuous rehearsal into a reason to keep pursuing an even more effortless appearance.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 supplies prize collection and a physical reception tactic; B4 locates A's rehearsal at a mirror.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A3's 'more effortless response' culminates economically in A5's competitive standard of looking unprepared.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 89\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"You looked so casual collecting that prize. I'll collect mine without even standing fully up.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"You haven't won one yet.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I'm preparing a more effortless response.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"You're practising it in a mirror.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Only until it looks less prepared than yours.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"retained\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"S205 makes another person's apparent ease both a model and a threat, driving A to outperform that very ease.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Vanity seeks an attractive appearance without necessarily borrowing a successful rival's confidence as its competitive target.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A1 names B's casual prize collection as the model, while A5's 'less prepared than yours' makes superiority over B the explicit goal.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"This alternative rehearses a future public success under a winner's skeptical eye; alternative b competes physically with a running companion who reveals hidden effort.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Retained unchanged: B2 turns A toward advance preparation and B4 makes that preparation the object of A5's renewed competition. Original reread supports the same internal pass.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"B primarily exposes contradictions rather than pursuing a separate activity; the scene passes because those corrections directly shape A's tactics.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S205-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"34318309d9f18753e25eb6898008671827e8356645a1cea9d85b546bbd3bd326\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 turns B's relaxed five miles into six smiling, but A5 defends appearances without sharpening the competitive target.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 uses B2's silence as a tactic; A5 reinterprets B4's breathlessness, though it adds no practical next move.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 explains a real running habit and B4 notices strain, but remains mostly a corrective observer.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A5 relabels exhaustion as restraint after B4 instead of increasing commitment to outperforming B.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes a five-mile run and a six-mile challenge; B4 supplies an immediately playable physical limitation.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3's energetic silence follows B2, but A5's 'restraint my effortless performance requires' is abstract and defensive.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 71\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"How do you finish five miles looking so relaxed? I'll do six smiling.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I'm exhausted. I just save my breath for the hill.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Then I'll save enough to congratulate you at the top.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"We can walk it together.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Lovely. I'll jog ahead and find us a bench. You make recovery look easy; I could have it ready for you.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"S205 makes A convert admiration of B's apparent composure into a need to demonstrate still greater capacity.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Simple exercise bravado boasts about endurance; this game specifically measures A against the ease success seems to give B.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Original A1 explicitly targets B's relaxed appearance; revised A5 turns B4's shared recovery offer into another opportunity to get ahead effortlessly.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a competes through rehearsed prize etiquette; this alternative turns a companion's helpful walking offer into competitive caretaking.\",\n      \"reason\": \"The original ended in a static excuse. The revision keeps running and adds a hill and bench. Separate internal reread: fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 4, heightening 4, specificity 5, brevity 4 = 92. B4's recovery offer prompts A5 to outperform B at arranging recovery.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A5's helpfulness must retain the comparison with B's apparent ease; otherwise the final offer could read as uncomplicated kindness.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S206-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"24fc19b6dcdae11fb8b38cd932f396b54c3cf3d5096b744fa77963634ec5b6f3\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 treats the three-year 